Directory: FISSION
File:      actinides_outer_saddles_Bf2_WMM.readme (24 May 2026)
****************************************************************

                     ACTINIDES_OUTER_SADDLES_BF2_WMM.DAT
 Second (outer) saddle-point properties and second (outer) fission barrier heights
                for 75 selected actinide isotopes with Z=89-98
        (Provided by P. Jachimowicz, M. Kowal, J. Skalski, 24 May 2026)
       *****************************************************************

Contents
--------
The file contains properties of the second (outer) saddle point and second (outer) fission barrier heights
for 75 selected actinide nuclei with Z=89-98. It provides masses, total energies, macroscopic and microscopic
energy contributions, the corresponding deformation parameters at the second (outer) saddle point,
and the calculated second (outer) fission barrier heights. A search for saddle points was performed using the
“imaginary water flow” method in a seven-dimensional deformation space including reflection-asymmetric shapes,
while nonaxial shapes were found to have a negligible effect in this case. For each set of deformations, the dipole (b1)
deformation parameter was fixed by requiring that the center of mass of the nuclear shape is at the origin;
thus, b1 is a function of the other deformation parameters. Further details of the model and computational procedure
can be found in Ref. [1].

Format
------
Each record of the file contains

     Z      : proton (charge) number
     N      : neutron number
     A      : mass number
     M      : saddle-point mass excess [MeV]
     Etot   : total saddle-point energy [MeV]
     Emac   : macroscopic saddle-point energy [MeV]
     Emic   : microscopic saddle-point energy [MeV]
     b1-b8  : deformation parameters at the second (outer) saddle point
     Bf2    : second (outer) fission barrier height [MeV]

The corresponding Fortran format specification is (3i4,13f8.2).

References
----------
[1] P. Jachimowicz, M. Kowal, J. Skalski, At. Data Nucl. Data Tables 138, 101393 (2021).
